{"title":"Strain-gradient elasticity","markdown":{"headingText":"Strain-gradient elasticity","headingAttr":{"id":"sec-gradient-elasticity","classes":[],"keyvalue":[]},"containsRefs":false,"markdown":"\n`apps/gradient_elasticity` — the one spectral application in the report that is\n**not** a time-stepping problem.\n\n## Physical setting\n\nClassical linear elasticity has no intrinsic length scale, so it predicts\nsingular strain fields at idealised defects — a dislocation core, a sharp crack\ntip — where the real material has a finite structure. Aifantis-type gradient\nelasticity introduces an internal length $\\ell$ that regularises exactly those\nfeatures while leaving the far field classical. It is the continuum answer to\n\"what happens below the scale where continuum elasticity stops making sense\".\n\n## Governing equations\n\nA Helmholtz operator applied to the Navier operator:\n\n$$\n\\big(1 - \\ell^2\\nabla^2\\big)\\,L_{\\mathrm{Navier}}\\,\\mathbf{u} \\;=\\; \\mathbf{f},\n$$ {#eq-ge-pde}\n\nwhich is fourth order in the displacement $\\mathbf{u}$. Features much smaller\nthan $\\ell$ are smoothed; features much larger than $\\ell$ are unaffected, and\nthe $\\ell \\to 0$ limit recovers classical elasticity.\n\n## Spectral formulation\n\nFor constant isotropic moduli on a periodic grid, @eq-ge-pde becomes an\nindependent $2\\times2$ linear system at each $\\mathbf{k}$ — no iteration and no\ntime stepping. Splitting the transformed body force into components parallel\nand perpendicular to $\\mathbf{k}$,\n\n$$\n\\hat{\\mathbf{u}}_L \\;=\\; \\frac{\\hat{\\mathbf{f}}_L}{-\\alpha(\\lambda+2\\mu)k^2},\n\\qquad\n\\hat{\\mathbf{u}}_T \\;=\\; \\frac{\\hat{\\mathbf{f}}_T}{-\\alpha\\mu k^2},\n$$ {#eq-ge-solve}\n\nwith the gradient factor\n\n$$\n\\alpha \\;=\\; 1 + \\ell^2 k^2 \\quad\\text{(fourth order)},\n$$ {#eq-ge-alpha}\n\nand optional sixth-order stretches $\\alpha = (1+\\ell^2k^2)^2$ or\n$\\alpha = 1 + \\ell^2k^2 + \\ell_4^4k^4$. The $\\mathbf{k}=0$ mode is projected to\n$\\hat{\\mathbf{u}} = 0$, which fixes the rigid-body translation the periodic\nproblem leaves undetermined.\n\nSeparating longitudinal from transverse components is what makes the system\ndiagonal: the two polarisations decouple, and each is a scalar divide.\n\n## Eigenstrain loading\n\nA two-dimensional dilatational eigenstrain\n$\\varepsilon^* = \\varepsilon_0\\,g\\,I$ is equivalent to the body force\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{f} \\;=\\; \\nabla\\cdot\\big(C:\\varepsilon^*\\big)\n            \\;=\\; 2(\\lambda+\\mu)\\,\\varepsilon_0\\,\\nabla g .\n$$ {#eq-ge-eigen}\n\nFor a cosine inclusion $g = A\\cos(\\mathbf{k}\\cdot\\mathbf{x})$ the displacement\nis the sine field $\\mathbf{u} = B\\,\\mathbf{k}\\sin(\\mathbf{k}\\cdot\\mathbf{x})$\nwith $B$ known in closed form — which is the analytical target the tests use.\n\n## Solution strategy\n\n**One shot, not ETD.** Forward transform the body force, solve @eq-ge-solve at\neach mode, inverse transform. Two transforms per component, no time loop, no\nstability condition.\n\nThis is worth stating plainly because it shows the spectral stack is not tied\nto time stepping: the same domain, field, and FFT machinery serves an elliptic\nsolve.\n\n## Verification\n\n* **Analytical displacement.** The computed $\\mathbf{u}$ matches the\n  closed-form Fourier solution for a cosine eigenstrain.\n* **Classical limit.** $\\ell \\to 0$ recovers the classical Navier result.\n* **High-$k$ reduction.** Short-wavelength response is suppressed by the\n  $\\alpha$ factor as designed.\n* **Zero mean.** $\\langle\\mathbf{u}\\rangle = 0$ exactly, from the\n  $\\mathbf{k}=0$ projection.\n\n## Binaries and inputs\n\n`gradient_elasticity` (CPU), `gradient_elasticity_hip`. Presets\n`inclusion.json`, `gaussian.json`.\n\n## Scalability\n\nNot measured. The cost is a fixed small number of transforms rather than a\ntime loop, so the relevant question is transform throughput rather than steps\nper second.\n","srcMarkdownNoYaml":""},"formats":{"html":{"identifier":{"display-name":"HTML","target-format":"html","base-format":"html"},"execute":{"fig-width":7,"fig-height":5,"fig-format":"retina","fig-dpi":96,"df-print":"default","error":false,"eval":true,"cache":null,"freeze":false,"echo":true,"output":true,"warning":true,"include":true,"keep-md":false,"keep-ipynb":false,"ipynb":null,"enabled":null,"daemon":null,"daemon-restart":false,"debug":false,"ipynb-filters":[],"ipynb-shell-interactivity":null,"plotly-connected":true,"engine":"markdown"},"render":{"keep-tex":false,"keep-typ":false,"keep-source":false,"keep-hidden":false,"prefer-html":false,"output-divs":true,"output-ext":"html","fig-align":"default","fig-pos":null,"fig-env":null,"code-fold":"none","code-overflow":"scroll","code-link":false,"code-line-numbers":false,"code-tools":false,"tbl-colwidths":"auto","merge-includes":true,"inline-includes":false,"preserve-yaml":false,"latex-auto-mk":true,"latex-auto-install":true,"latex-clean":true,"latex-min-runs":1,"latex-max-runs":10,"latex-makeindex":"makeindex","latex-makeindex-opts":[],"latex-tlmgr-opts":[],"latex-input-paths":[],"latex-output-dir":null,"link-external-icon":false,"link-external-newwindow":false,"self-contained-math":false,"format-resources":[],"notebook-links":true},"pandoc":{"standalone":true,"wrap":"none","default-image-extension":"png","to":"html","toc":true,"number-sections":true,"html-math-method":"mathjax","output-file":"11_gradient_elasticity.html"},"language":{"toc-title-document":"Table of contents","toc-title-website":"On this page","related-formats-title":"Other Formats","related-notebooks-title":"Notebooks","source-notebooks-prefix":"Source","other-links-title":"Other Links","code-links-title":"Code Links","launch-dev-container-title":"Launch Dev Container","launch-binder-title":"Launch Binder","article-notebook-label":"Article Notebook","notebook-preview-download":"Download Notebook","notebook-preview-download-src":"Download Source","notebook-preview-back":"Back to Article","manuscript-meca-b